Design:
- Use the 1D reduction: ψ(t,r) = r φ(t,r) satisfies ψ_tt = ψ_rr on r>0 with Dirichlet ψ(t,0)=0 (regularity at origin for ℓ=0). Closed-form: d'Alembert ψ = f(r−t)+g(r+t) with ψ(t,0)=0 ⇒ g = −f(−·)? Let's pick a smooth closed-form: ψ(t,r) = sin(k r) cos(k t)? Check: ψ_tt = −k² sin(kr) cos(kt); ψ_rr = −k² sin(kr) cos(kt). Yes! And ψ(t,0)=0. Then φ = sin(kr)cos(kt)/r. Domain r ∈ [0, R], with outgoing/incoming exact. Or use a traveling Gaussian pulse: ψ(t,r) = A exp(−(r−t−r0)²/σ²) − A exp(−(−r−t−r0)²/σ²) to enforce ψ(t,0)=0 (antisymmetric mirror). That's a standard exact solution in the domain r≥0 with Dirichlet at 0. But a smooth sine standing wave is simpler and has zero at origin; the exact solution is exact for all time in [0,R] including reflection at origin (the Dirichlet condition makes the antisymmetric extension exact). With ψ = sin(kr)cos(kt), ψ_tt = ψ_rr exactly; ψ(t,0)=0 exactly; the numerical scheme with Dirichlet at r=0 and R will approximate it.

Convergence order: second-order central differences in space and time (explicit leapfrog): 
ψ^{n+1}_j = 2ψ^n_j − ψ^{n−1}_j + λ²(ψ^n_{j+1} − 2ψ^n_j + ψ^n_{j−1}), λ = dt/dr, with CFL λ ≤ 1. Choose λ = 0.5 fixed (or exact to declared). Error O(dr²) in space and time if dt ∝ dr. Measure order via Richardson: log2(||e_{h}|| / ||e_{h/2}||) using L2 or L∞ norm at fixed final time. Need 3+ resolutions: e.g., N = 100, 200, 400 (or 64/128/256). measured order between successive pairs; assert min(orders) ≥ 2 − tol (tol e.g. 0.15), and exit code 0 only if all pass.

